<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.12.4: Representations of Quadratic Functions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01952</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.12.4: Representations of Quadratic Functions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>bcb1aaed-5eb5-437b-8c6e-ba4a05bdadf4</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">      <title>Activity</title>

      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00001">Your teacher will give your group a <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/e7d49f9b3abc6ab2f74cf7dec6b56de506a27407">set
            of cards</link>. Each card contains a graph or an equation.</para>
            <list id="list-00001">
          <item><para id="para-00002">Take turns with your partner to sort the cards into sets so that
            each set contains two equations and a graph that all represent the same quadratic
            function.</para></item>
          <item><para id="para-00003">For each set of cards that you put together, explain to your partner
            how you know they belong together.</para></item>
          <item><para id="para-00004">For each set that your partner puts together, listen carefully to
            their explanation. If you disagree, discuss your thinking and work to reach an
            agreement.</para></item>
          </list>
          <para id="para-00005">Once all the cards are sorted and discussed, record the equivalent
            equations, sketch the corresponding graph, and write a brief note or explanation about
            why the representations were grouped together.</para>
          <para id="para-00006">Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis>
            button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00007">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00008">Set 1:  Standard Form: <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> Factored Form: <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00009">
            <media alt="A parabola on a coordinate grid. The x-axis scale is 1 and extends from negative 4 to 6. The y-axis scale is 1 and extends from negative 6 to 7.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_14_09_01.svg" width="264"/>
            </media>

          </para>
          <para id="para-00010">Set 2: Standard Form: <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> Factored Form: <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00011">
            <media alt="A parabola on a coordinate grid. The x-axis scale is 1 and extends from negative 4 to 6. The y-axis scale is 1 and extends from negative 6 to 7.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_14_09_02.svg" width="264"/>
            </media>

          </para>
          <para id="para-00012">Set 3: Standard Form: <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> Factored Form: <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00013">
            <media alt="A parabola on a coordinate grid. The x-axis scale is 1 and extends from negative 4 to 6. The y-axis scale is 1 and extends from negative 6 to 7.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_14_09_03.svg" width="264"/>
            </media>

          </para>
          <para id="para-00014">Set 4: Standard Form: <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> Factored Form: <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00015">
            <media alt="A parabola on a coordinate grid. The x-axis scale is 1 and extends from negative 4 to 6. The y-axis scale is 1 and extends from negative 6 to 7.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_14_09_04.svg" width="264"/>
            </media>

          </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

    </section>

    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00016"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_7_12_4_SC"/></para>
        </problem>
      </exercise>
    </note>

    <section id="sect-00003">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00002">
        <title>Matching Quadratic Functions and Their Graphs</title>
        <para id="para-00019">Below is a quadratic function represented in standard form, factored
          form, and with a graph.</para>
        <para id="para-00020">Standard Form: <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>f</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:msup>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>8</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para><para id="para-00017">Factored Form: <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>f</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>4</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00021">
          <media alt="A parabola on a coordinate grid. The x-axis scale is 1 and extends from negative 10 to 10. The y-axis scale is 1 and extends from negative 14 to 6.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_14_11_01.jpg" width="300"/>
          </media>

        </para>
        <para id="para-00022">Notice the factored form helps find the zeros: <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>4</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>.</para>
        <para id="para-00023">The factored form gives the zeros since the zeros (<m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>-intercepts) are located where
          each factor equals 0.</para>
        <para id="para-00024">The standard form, <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>f</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:msup>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>8</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>, helps give the <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>-intercept. The <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>-intercept occurs when <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>0</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>. Here, <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>f</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mn>0</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>8</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>. On the graph, the <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>-intercept is <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mn>0</m:mn>
              <m:mo>,</m:mo>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>8</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>.</para>
        <note class="try" id="note-00002">
          <title>Try It: Matching Quadratic Functions and Their Graphs</title>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00002">
            <problem id="prob-00002">
              <para id="para-00025">Given the graph below and its function in standard form and
          factored form, identify all intercepts.</para>
              <para id="para-00026">
                <media alt="A parabola on a coordinate grid. The x-axis scale is 1 and extends from negative 10 to 10. The y-axis scale is 1 and extends from negative 14 to 6.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_14_11_02.jpg" width="300"/>
                </media>

              </para>
              <para id="para-00027">Standard form: <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    </m:msup>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math> Factored
          form: <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></para>
              <para id="para-00028">Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis>
          button to compare your work.</para>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00002">
              <para id="para-00029">Here is how to find the intercepts with the information
          provided:</para>
              <para id="para-00030">The graph and the factored form help give the <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>-intercepts.
          They occur where the factors of the factored form equal 0, at <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>. On the
          graph, these are where the graph crosses the <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>-axis, at the
          points <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>
          and <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>
          .</para>
              <para id="para-00031">The <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>-intercept can
          be found with standard form when <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math> in <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    </m:msup>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>. So <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>. Notice on
          the graph, the <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>-intercept,
          where the graph crosses the <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>-axis, is at <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>
          .</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
        </note>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>